another active learning technique that  can be very very effective in supporting  student learning is the jigsaw and it  gets its name from the from the the  activity involved when you do a jigsaw  puzzle and usually most people when they  do a jigsaw will look for the the board  they'll find all the edge pieces or  they'll find all the blue pieces or  they'll find all the pieces that have  have a certain image on them you do the  homogeneous aspects of the exercise so  you get all the edge pieces the blue  pieces the the particular image pieces  but eventually you have to take those  pieces and put them together you have to  link them up with pieces that don't look  quite like those pieces so that's the  heterogeneous component of it and and so  what you do in a jigsaw is you ask  students to become experts if you will  homogeneous experts in a particular area  on a particular topic and you can do  this by giving them a reading in class  well you can do this by asking to  reflect on something that you've you've  addressed in in a previous class or that  they've addressed in a previous homework  but but they sit together or they  collect they they group together in this  what I call homogeneous group where  everyone is talking about the same  particular topic and they just discuss  that topic and they become experts in  that homogeneous topic and then you can  have three or four different topics  whatever depending on the sizes size of  the class and you have these local  groups of experts like the edge pieces  and the blue pieces and them and the  picture pieces and then after giving  them a period of time maybe five minutes  ten minutes depending on the class you  break them up into heterogeneous groups  so you get one one representative from  Group A goes with one representative  from Group B and one representative come  from C and one representative from Group  D and now you have a new heterogeneous  group that has an A or B a C and D in it  or if we use the puzzle analogy you have  a group that has some person that's the  edge expert some persons that's the  picture expert in some person that's the  blue piece expert  and they're all in a group and now they  share what they know individually with  each other and then they combine that  expertise they synthesize that expertise  to make something bigger to come up with  a global under understanding of the  problem and then each group does that in  their own way so the heterogeneous  groups then form a more holistic or a  more heterogeneous solution to the  problem as if they're putting together  the different kinds of puzzle pieces and  then again as with the other activities  we ask them to to then report out before  that I usually ask I usually will  circulate in the room the same way I do  with the pair share to hear what each of  the groups are saying to see if any  groups are having particular issues or  if they have some particular  breakthroughs that I want to make sure  they share with with the class as a  whole there's some logistical issues one  you want to make sure that the students  really can become these homogeneous  experts so if you have a group of four  people that they've all really come up  to speed on the topic so ideally you  would give a pre class assignment and  you'd say yes I need everybody to read  this article and to be able to explain  this point this point in this point and  you would find that - that group that a  particular group of students and then  you do the same with a few other topics  you you're assuming then that the  students have read the paper or Rett  have read the article whatever it is you  want them to read and that they  understand it to the level that you hope  they understood it sometimes you don't  have time for that or it just doesn't  work out so you give them something to  read within in class and again that can  be tricky students may not have the same  level of understanding so groups may be  a little bit spotty the other thing that  can happen with the jigsaw which is like  a really silly problem but it but  sometimes it just trips you up is if you  have depending on a number of students  you have to kind of think on your feet  about how many students are in the  homogeneous group versus how many  students are in the heterogeneous group  if it's a perfect square it works fine  so if you've got nine students or  sixteen students or or twenty-five  students you know you have five groups  of five groups in one situation than  five groups in the other that means you  have to have the same number of groups  as heterogeneous topics so if I went  ABCD I have to then have four groups  because I had to have four A's for B's  for season four DS so sometimes just the  number of topics and the number of  students that you have in your class  makes it kind of hard to make groups  that don't have extra people or aren't  short a particular expert and that's  just something you want to plan  beforehand and if somebody doesn't come  to class you know if you know you have  25 students in your class and someone  doesn't show up then you have to kind of  deal with it on this on the fly but that  can be the biggest that can be the  trickiest thing when the jigsaw is just  getting the number students right
 

